-- title:
--   Hecke eigensystem representation with a Frobenius-stable line at q
-- statement:
--   Let $p$ be a prime, $N \ge 1$, and $S$ a finite set of natural numbers containing $p$ and every prime divisor of $N$. Let $\mathcal O'$ be a complete discrete valuation ring (a domain, $\mathfrak m$-adically complete, with finite residue field) of characteristic zero in which $p$ lies in the maximal ideal, and let $\chi$ be a ring homomorphism from [`CuspForm.heckeAlgebra N 2 S`](def/CuspForm_HeckeAlgebra.html#L18) — the $\mathbb Z$-subalgebra of $\operatorname{End}_{\mathbb C}(S_2(\Gamma_0(N)))$ generated by the operators `heckeTLin` at primes $\ell \nmid N$ with $\ell \notin S$ together with the operators `heckeULin` at primes dividing $N$ outside $S$ — to $\mathcal O'$. Let $q$ be a prime with $q \nmid N$ and $q \notin S$, and let $\alpha \in \mathcal O'$ satisfy $\alpha^2 - \chi(T_q)\alpha + q = 0$. Then there exist a ring $\mathcal O''$ of the same kind (a characteristic-zero complete discrete valuation domain with finite residue field), an $\mathcal O'$-algebra structure on $\mathcal O''$ making it a finite $\mathcal O'$-module with $\operatorname{algebraMap}$ a local homomorphism and injective, and a [`GaloisRepAdic 𝒪''`](def/GaloisRep_Adic.html#L16), i.e. a free $\mathcal O''$-module $V$ of rank $2$ with a monoid homomorphism $\rho$ from $\operatorname{Gal}(\overline{\mathbb Q}/\mathbb Q)$ (the group of $\mathbb Q$-algebra automorphisms of $\operatorname{AlgebraicClosure} \mathbb Q$) to $\operatorname{End}_{\mathcal O''}(V)$ which is $\mathfrak m$-adically continuous in the sense that for each $n$ some finite extension of $\mathbb Q$ inside $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ has the property that elements fixing it act trivially on $V$ modulo $\mathfrak m^n V$, such that: (i) for every prime $\ell \nmid N$ with $\ell \notin S$, every valuation subring $A$ of $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ with $\ell$ a non-unit of $A$, and every $\sigma$ lying in the decomposition group of $A$ and inducing $x \mapsto x^{\ell}$ on the residue field of $A$, the characteristic polynomial of $\rho(\sigma)$ is $X^2 - \chi(T_\ell)X + \ell$ (images taken in $\mathcal O''$); (ii) for every prime $r \nmid N$ with $r \ne p$ and every valuation subring over $r$, all elements of the image of the inertia subgroup act as the identity on $V$; and (iii) for every valuation subring $P$ of $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ with $q$ a non-unit of $P$ there is an $\mathcal O''$-submodule $L \subseteq V$ which is the span of the first vector of some basis of $V$ indexed by $\operatorname{Fin} 2$, is stable under the decomposition group of $P$, satisfies $\rho(\tau)v - v \in L$ for every $\tau$ in the image of the inertia subgroup and every $v \in V$, satisfies $\rho(\sigma)v - \alpha v \in L$ for every $v \in V$ and every Frobenius $\sigma$ at $P$ over $q$, and on $L$ itself every such $\sigma$ acts as multiplication by $\chi(T_q) - \alpha$.
--
--   This is local–global compatibility at a prime $q$ not dividing the level, in the shape needed for level raising: the representation attached to a weight-two Hecke eigensystem of level $N$ has, at each place above $q$, a line stable under the decomposition group on which Frobenius acts by $\chi(T_q)-\alpha$ and with unramified quotient on which it acts by the chosen root $\alpha$ of $X^2 - \chi(T_q)X + q$. It is used in the passage to the $q$-stabilised situation, where $\alpha$ is a unit root and becomes the $U_q$-eigenvalue at level $Nq$.
